    "created_at": "2023-03-21T15:47:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-03-21T15:47:33Z",
    "body": "> Turns out that [the intent of `:open`](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/7319#issuecomment-1242377579) is not just to match open popovers, but also other kinds of openness, such as `<details open>` (thanks @bkardell for sending me the resolution). So `element.matches(':open')` isn't an effective way to detect if the element is an open popover.\r\n> \r\n> There are even cases where a single element, such as `<details popover>`, can be two kinds of `:open` at the same time.\r\n\r\nThis is a good point, and a good case for adding `popoverOpen`. You could always use `.matches(':open:not([open])')` but that's a mouthful and not ergonomic.\r\n\r\nI'm supportive of adding `popoverOpen`, [defined](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9045#issuecomment-1476981036) roughly as:\r\n> The popoverOpen IDL attribute returns true if the element's [popover visibility state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#popover-visibility-state) is [showing](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#popover-showing-state), or false otherwise. \r\n\r\nIn other words, this isn't sugar for `.matches(':open')` but is specific to Popovers that are open **as a popover**, which allows you to more easily disambiguate the `<dialog popover>` and `<details popover>` cases.\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
